resource budget (§9). |\r\n246-| **Conforming** | Uses the statement pattern below and the §2 id scheme. |\r\n247-| **Traceable** | Carries a stable unique id and its parent/child links (§2; the matrix above). |\r\n248-\r\n249-**The statement pattern is EARS** (Easy Approach to Requirements Syntax): the\r\n250-condition goes **in front of** the subject, so a reader learns *when the\r\n251-requirement applies* before *what it obliges*. One pattern per row:\r\n252-\r\n253-| Pattern | Grammar | Use when |\r\n254-|---|---|---|\r\n255-| Ubiquitous | `The <system> shall <response>.` | It always holds. |\r\n256-| Event-driven | `When <trigger>, the <system> shall <response>.` | A discrete event starts it. |\r\n257-| State-driven | `While <state>, the <system> shall <response>.` | It holds for the duration of a state. |\r\n--\r\n384-contract.\r\n385-\r\n386-**Routing (where the map lands).** The map's ONE rendered home is the\r\n387:dashboard's \"How (SW architecture)\" tab, derived live from the source AST —\r\n388-no committed markdown copy to rot. `gen_arch_map.py --doc` (repeatable) can\r\n389-ALSO splice it *directly into `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`* (zero\r\n390-hops, but the guide's diff churns with the code; good when the map fits on a\r\n391-screen); there `--check` fails the gate if stale, so it never rots. Don't\r\n392-hand-maintain a code map.\r\n393-\r\n394-**The derived map is a contract, not a search index.** The AST inventory is\r\n395-**derived, deterministic and drift-proof** — the dashboard and the checks read\r\n396-the source itself, learning the code's *actual* shape. Query-time\r\n397-**semantic-retrieval tools** (LSP-backed code-graph servers, Serena-style MCP\r\n398-indexes) are a *different* thing: not committed, language-server-dependent,\r\n399-rebuilt on demand. They are a legitimate **optional downstream accelerator** for\r\n--\r\n602-so its one input is the harness-written `docs/test/evidence` record\r\n603-(`record_test_evidence.py`), bound by value to the tree it measured.\r\n604-\r\n605:**Requirements come before architecture**, because architecture is a *response*\r\n606-to requirements: a scope is partitioned in whatever way best satisfies its\r\n607-obligations, so partitioning first means partitioning against nothing but the\r\n608-frame. An interface and a requirement say different things — an interface says\r\n609-*what crosses* (provider, consumers, contract, type), a requirement says\r\n610-*what must be achieved* — so neither derives from the other.\r\n611-\r\n612-**The frame is a registry, not prose.** `DevStg-Boundary`'s deliverable is\r\n613-`requirements/external.toml`: who is outside (`[entity.EXT-###]`), what\r\n614-crosses the boundary (`[boundary.B-##]`), and the external-to-external flows\r\n615-the system is *not* a party to (`[relationship.REL-###]`). An SR states an\r\n616-observable **at** a crossing and cites it (`boundary_refs`); an IF row that\r\n617-realizes a crossing ties back to it directionally (§8).\r\n--\r\n1104-`scripts/check` (and the CI workflow) must run, and fail nonzero on any failure:\r\n1105-format check · linter (warnings as errors) · unit + integration tests · coverage\r\n1106-(≥ threshold) · the traceability check (0 orphans at the derived gate). Emit the\r\n1107:coverage + traceability reports as artifacts. The architecture views are\r\n1108-derived from source + registries, so no map step is owed.\r\n1109-\r\n1110-**The derived gate is cached, and CI reads it.** The rung the repo is IN\r\n1111-lives in `docs/stage` — **generated** by\r\n1112-`scripts/derive_stage.py` from the artifact states, not hand-set (§4 \"Stages and\r\n1113-gates\"; the model:\r\n1114-[process-options.md](process-options.md#derived-gate-model)). `check.py` defaults\r\n1115-`--stage` to it and selects by rung or declared paths (below), so **CI enforces the\r\n1116-bar the project has actually earned** — a fresh scaffold deriving DevStg-Reqs is\r\n1117-green, and more steps select when a batch of artifacts is **approved in a reviewed\r\n1118-commit** and `docs/stage` is regenerated. The `derived-stage` step\r\n1119-(`derive_stage.py --check`) guards the cache against rot on every trunk-lane\r\n--\r\n1261-  `--strict`), **not** wired into `check.py`'s required floor. A Python project runs\r\n1262-  it to inform the DevStg-Impl Inspection; a non-Python stack swaps or drops it.\r\n1263-- `scripts/gen_arch_map.py` — the module/function AST walk the derived\r\n1264:  architecture reads (`scan_inventory`: summaries, `Implements:` back-links,\r\n1265-  imports, seams); its CLI splices the rendered map + Mermaid **dependency\r\n1266-  diagram** into opt-in `--doc` marker blocks, where `--check` fails on drift\r\n1267-  and `--strict-parse` fails on any module that won't parse.\r\n1268-- `scripts/gen_release_checklist.py` — generates the human **release checklist**\r\n1269-  for `DevStg-Release` from the registries: every Demonstration/Manual/Inspection SR,\r\n1270-  every Release-tier/manual TC, the SN acceptance intents, and provided\r\n1271:  interfaces — each a tick-box back-linked to its id. Keep the completed copy as\r\n1272-  the sign-off record.\r\n1273-- `scripts/gen_cases.py` — expands an SR's `Permutations` (input dimensions) into\r\n1274-  boundary-aware test combinations by strategy (full / pairwise / boundaries),\r\n1275-  and reports the reduction vs. the full product (see \"Dimensional coverage\" in\r\n1276-  §4). Use it at DevStg-Tests to design test cases that exercise the input space.\r\n1277-\r\n1278-**Cross-platform launchers** (so a fresh clone is trivial to run on any OS):\r\n1279-`scripts/setup.{sh,ps1}` create a venv and install the toolchain;\r\n1280-`scripts/check.{sh,ps1}` are thin wrappers that forward to `check.py`. Provide\r\n1281-the pair for every platform the project supports.\r\n1282-\r\n1283-`scripts/bootstrap.py` scaffolds all of the above (plus `docs/` and CI) into a new\r\n--\r\n1287-\r\n1288-When this project provides or consumes a surface — shared with another repo, or\r\n1289-between its **own modules** — record each seam once in\r\n1290:`requirements/interfaces.toml` as an `IF-###` (see `INTERFACES.template.md`).\r\n1291-**One row is one owner, its far side, and a typed statement of the\r\n1292-information.** `Owner` is the providing THING — what the information plugs\r\n1293-into — in the one spelling the far side uses: a module path, a file or\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
